WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:03.362
When we write reusable functions, we should explain them.

00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:10.497
A docstring is a short explanation placed inside a function, usually right after the function definition.

00:00:10.497 --> 00:00:16.027
A good docstring tells the reader what the function does, what inputs it expects, what it returns,

00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:19.692
and any important assumptions.

00:00:19.692 --> 00:00:25.112
In this example, the function conversion_rate calculates a signup conversion rate.

00:00:25.112 --> 00:00:29.382
The docstring explains that visits means the total number of visitors,

00:00:29.382 --> 00:00:34.892
signups means the number of people who signed up, and the return value is a percentage.

00:00:34.892 --> 00:00:38.592
The function also handles the case where visits is zero.

00:00:38.592 --> 00:00:44.057
If visits is zero, the function returns zero instead of dividing by zero.

00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:47.482
Run the cell and notice that it prints the conversion rate.

00:00:47.482 --> 00:00:50.067
Then it calls help on the function.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.962
The help output displays the docstring.

00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:55.922
This is one reason docstrings are useful.

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:59.032
They are not only comments for people reading the file.

00:00:59.032 --> 00:01:01.893
They can also be displayed by Python tools.
